      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "In questionnaire surveys, patients with chronic heart failure frequently report \"pain\" as a symptom. We investigated the prevalence of chest pain as a possible cause for pain, particularly in patients with prior myocardial infarction."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Questionnaire survey."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Community heart failure clinic."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS",
            "content": "1 786 patients with heart failure due to left ventricular systolic dysfunction (mean ± SD age 70.1 ± 11.0 years; 73% male; left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) 35.3 ± 9.9%; 65.6 with underlying ischaemic heart disease (IHD))."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTION",
            "content": "Patients with chronic heart failure completed a questionnaire."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Answers to the questions: (1) \"In the last week, how many days did you get angina chest pain?\"; and \"In the last month, how much did the following affect you:\" (2) \"chest pains at rest\"; (3) \"chest pains during normal activity\"."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "73% of those with IHD, and 84% of those without had had no angina in the previous week; 79% and 82%, respectively, had at most \"little\" chest pain at rest; 67% and 76%, respectively, had at most \"little\" chest pain during exertion. Angina increased with NYHA class, but there was no relation between angina and sex of patient, age or LVEF. There was a weak relation between chest pain and an adverse outcome in the patients with ischaemic heart disease."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Although pain is commonly reported in patients with chronic heart failure, it seems unlikely that the pain is due to angina, even in patients with underlying coronary heart disease."
          }
        ]
      },
